From bdeba5b458f40cbc7375add18887490967d45ea3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 09:10:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Pin the A/B item 3-8a rests on, after the build stopped

3-8a was taken to the build and is not built. Two things came out of the
attempt.

The mechanism is an XL after all, and the scoping that called it an M was
wrong. Narrowing which names the item speculates on does not narrow what
has to change to hold one: a local that is a raw double advertises itself
through VariableScope.NumericStorage, and every fast path in the compiler
keys off that one field -- item 3-0's numeric index, item 3-5's mixed
comparison, the raw store, the native update step, and
ToNativeExpression's "this leaf is already a double". A speculative local
is a double only while a flag holds, so each of those becomes guard-aware
or reads a dead double, and a site that is missed is a wrong answer
rather than a slow one.

The population could not be measured, which is what actually stopped it.
The instrument took the same optimistic fixed point a second time with a
name from an enclosing scope assumed numeric and subtracted the real
survivors, so the set came out of the existing analysis by difference
rather than out of a new rule. It read 0 on all seven Octane suites and
0 on the shape it was built for, so it never discriminated, and a counter
never shown to read non-zero is a claim about the counter. One real
defect fell out of it on the way, and it is 0083's a second time: the
enable for a compile-time counter was placed among the run-time censuses,
which are switched on after the corpus has already compiled. Fixing the
placement changed nothing, so the instrument was reverted rather than
shipped.

What is kept is the A/B the item rests on, reduced to a single
difference: `var c = 2 * rowSize; c++` is a LocalSlot whose every step
boxes, and `var c = 2 * 10; c++` is numeric and costs nothing. Same
nesting, same body, same update, one identifier different -- so the
enclosing-scope read is not one defeat among several on that shape, it is
the defeat. The control is the interesting half: without it the slot
would be consistent with any of the six other conjuncts refusing the
local.

Tests only; no engine change.
---
 .../NumericLocalDefeatTests.cs                | 40 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Broiler.JS/Broiler.JavaScript.Compiler.Tests/NumericLocalDefeatTests.cs b/Broiler.JS/Broiler.JavaScript.Compiler.Tests/NumericLocalDefeatTests.cs
index 43fb5898..504752dc 100644
--- a/Broiler.JS/Broiler.JavaScript.Compiler.Tests/NumericLocalDefeatTests.cs
+++ b/Broiler.JS/Broiler.JavaScript.Compiler.Tests/NumericLocalDefeatTests.cs
@@ -200,4 +200,44 @@ public sealed class NumericLocalDefeatTests
         Assert.Equal(1, Dropped(d, NumericDropCause.Parameter));
         Assert.Equal(0, Dropped(d, NumericDropCause.OtherName));
     }
+
+    [Fact]
+    public void TheEnclosingNameIsTheBindingConstraintAndOneCharacterProvesIt()
+    {
+        // The A/B item 3-8a's population rests on, reduced to a single difference. Two programs
+        // whose inner function is identical except that the initializer reads a name from the
+        // enclosing scope in one and a literal in the other:
+        //
+        //     var c = 2 * rowSize;   ->  LocalSlot, and every c++ boxes
+        //     var c = 2 * 10;        ->  numeric, and c++ costs nothing
+        //
+        // Everything else about the two is the same — same nesting, same enclosing declaration,
+        // same body, same update. So the enclosing-scope read is not one defeat among several on
+        // this shape, it is THE defeat, and it is what 3-8a proposes to test at run time.
+        //
+        // Kept as a pair rather than as one assertion because the interesting half is the control:
+        // a fixture that only showed the slot would be consistent with the local being refused for
+        // any of the six other conjuncts, which is exactly the ambiguity the rest of this file
+        // exists to remove.
+        var viaEnclosingName = Analyse("""
+            function o(){
+              var rowSize = 10;
+              function f(){ var c = 2 * rowSize; c++; return c; }
+              return f();
+            }
+            o();
+            """);
+
+        Assert.Equal(1, Target(viaEnclosingName, ArithmeticOperandDiagnostics.UpdateTarget.LocalSlot));
+
+        var viaLiteral = Analyse("""
+            function o(){
+              function f(){ var c = 2 * 10; c++; return c; }
+              return f();
+            }
+            o();
+            """);
+
+        Assert.Equal(0, viaLiteral.Steps);
+    }
 }
